WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:07.399
Right.

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:09.950
The next item is a report on Winterbourne
View.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:10.950
Jane?

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.070
Jane Cummings: Thank you.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:18.510
The Board will know that I have previously
reported on progress, or lack of progress,

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.750
with the Winterbourne View concordat.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:25.220
Since our last meeting the 1st June has passed.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:31.919
That was the date that was agreed, as part
of the Winterbourne View concordat, all appropriate

00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:34.410
patients would be discharged from inpatient
settings.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:40.890
However, as everybody knows and has been widely
reported, that date was not delivered.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:46.740
I think that continues to be a significant
concern to those of us round this table.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.150
More importantly, it continues to be a significant
concern to people that are in inpatient settings

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.730
and their loved ones.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.690
We have put a considerable amount of effort.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.500
First, that effort has gone into improving
our delivery of those recommendations and

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.500
actions.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.470
It has also gone into thinking differently
about how we move forward.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:15.069
It is clearly not straightforward and it is
clearly not something that can be done very

00:01:15.069 --> 00:01:16.069
easily.

00:01:16.069 --> 00:01:19.869
If it was, I am sure it would have been managed
better by now.

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:22.080
We have reviewed what we have been doing.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:28.179
We have changed some of our internal governance
processes.

00:01:28.179 --> 00:01:35.219
We have made very specific and detailed efforts
to engage much more widely across our system

00:01:35.219 --> 00:01:39.229
through, for example, the commissioning operations
directorate.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:45.119
CCGs and area teams have had very clear expectations
set out about what we want to see delivered.

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:49.789
That information is contained in the paper.

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:59.109
We have also now completed, as planned, a
review of each of the 47 patients that were

00:01:59.109 --> 00:02:00.810
resident at Winterbourne View.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.380
That review has been carried out by our Improving
Lives team.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:05.950
They are clinical experts in learning disability.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.920
I think they have had significant success.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:15.630
They have worked with key partners, individuals,
the people and their families.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.510
They have been very well received.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:22.500
Furthermore, as a result of that review we
have transferred patients out that were otherwise

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.640
considered to be in long term inpatient settings.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.330
They are now being quite sought after to provide
advice and guidance.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:38.450
We are, therefore, helping to support those
key staff to continue to provide overview

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.460
of patients with particularly complex conditions.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:47.670
I think at the last meeting we also reported
that we were waiting for individual plans

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:53.560
from CCGs and specialised commissioning teams
about potential discharge dates.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:59.600
The results of those plans are that we have
about a third of the inpatients with a planned

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.410
date for discharge over the next three, six
or 12 months.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.100
On one level, that is some progress.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:13.530
However, I imagine all of you would agree
with me, that is just not good enough.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.500
It is not where we would want to be.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:21.270
Although I recognise there is progress made,
we still want to go further.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:28.370
Therefore, we have been looking as to how
we can avoid admission in the first place,

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:33.830
promote early discharge and provide much better
community services.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:41.700
As a result of working with third sector organisations
we have had three different sets of organisations

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:48.210
that have provided us with ideas and suggestions
about how we can take that forward.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.060
We are proposing we establish a sort of strategic
steering group.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.720
Hopefully the Board will support this proposal.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:04:00.110
This strategic steering group will oversee
the vision and strategy for how we can commission

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:01.110
differently.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:05.810
They will work in partnership with third sector
organisations and other experts in this field.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:12.590
The aim is to really have a step change, in
a completely different way and move it onto

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:14.050
a different level, I suppose.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:17.039
Clearly there has been quite a lot of work
done.

00:04:17.039 --> 00:04:19.900
We are beginning to see progress being made.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:24.450
However, I think most people would suggest
that is not fast enough and we want to go

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:25.450
further.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:31.560
We also know that families, carers, a lot
of our third sector organisations, local government

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.570
associations and in fact NHS England itself
would welcome the opportunity to do things

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:37.570
differently.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:42.460
I am really pleased that Sir Stephen Bubb,
the Chief Executive of ACEVO, has agreed to

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:44.460
chair that group.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.110
That group will involve key partners and key
stakeholders.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:52.810
They will include clinicians and things like
LGA.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:05:00.780
We would aim to have an agreed full membership
of that group and a timeline for delivery

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.889
identified in the very near future.

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:10.400
We will look to promulgate, actually expand
on and communicate that timeline widely with

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:17.230
key stakeholders, families and other key partners.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:18.290
That is a very brief overview.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.160
The paper is as you have it.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:26.150
I would be very happy to take any questions
and would welcome comments from the Board.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:31.680
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Jane.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.430
Are there any questions and comments?

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:42.750
Margaret Casely Hayford: Thank you very much,
Chairman.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:49.480
For me, the really critical point here is
what happens with advocacy.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:53.150
Essentially, I think people feeling that they
were not listened to, and indeed not being

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:58.750
listened to or heard, was previously a huge
gap in process and procedure.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:07.360
I recognise there is going to be scrutiny
on the quality of advocacy.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:15.560
However, I would like to know what the oversight
looks like.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:22.690
Can you give a bit more detail about the process
around how we would oversee the improvement

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:28.160
in quality of advocacy and also the response
and listening aspect?

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:38.620
Jane Cummings: The approach we are proposing
is almost a two pronged approach to this.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:42.770
The sort of strategic steering group I have
just talked about is very much about what

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:46.050
the new future commissioning model will look
like.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.020
I think it is important that the group concentrates
on that.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:54.250
However, the group must also make sure there
is very strong liaison and communication with

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:58.430
some of the other governance arrangements
we have in place.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:01.820
Alongside that, we will continue to deliver
the on going, day to day, improvements we

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:02.820
are making.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.840
That will be done through our programme Board,
which is the joint improvement Board that

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.020
actually already exists.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.820
Advocacy is part of those improvements.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:20.860
The advocacy role is also the responsibility
of local government.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:25.340
About 97% of people have advocates.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.870
I think we would like to see that strengthened,
in terms of the role.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:33.400
That is not to say the advocates that are
there are not, in many cases, doing a good

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:34.400
job.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.420
However, we want the ability for individuals
and their families to really feel they have

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:44.010
somebody who will act on their behalf, put
a challenge into the system and call for additional

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:45.010
help and advice.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.780
That additional help and advice may particularly
come from the Improving Lives team, for example.

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:53.699
Furthermore, we have the ability to be able
to provide almost an independent assessment

00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:57.320
of some of those patients who have been in
hospital for a considerable length time.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:00.919
What is it that is stopping us moving them?

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:06.690
What is it about clinical practice, the local
commissioning arrangements or the local services

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:13.620
that is actually stopping us moving people
if they should come out of an inpatient setting.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:18.000
I suppose the short answer to your questions,
as opposed to the slightly longer answer I

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.820
have just given, is that I would say: we will
use the existing systems.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:26.930
We will use the joint improvement Board and
we will link that to the over arching governance

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:28.100
arrangements we have in place.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.959
That will allow us to make sure we have a
good accountability for all of the actions

00:08:32.959 --> 00:08:33.969
that are set out in Winterbourne View.

00:08:33.969 --> 00:08:37.899
Simon Stevens: Can I just underline one point
Jane has just made?

00:08:37.899 --> 00:08:43.580
In addition to the advocacy services, I think
our aim is to get in place an independent

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:44.770
second opinion service.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:45.770
Jane Cummings: Yes.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:48.830
Simon Stevens: Every individual affected by
the programme can have access to that service.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:53.790
That will mean they are not only reliant on
the clinical advice they are getting from

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.790
their local area.

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:59.820
Are you able to say anything about what the
process will be?

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:03.210
Are there timelines for putting in place that
second opinion service?

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:11.130
Jane Cummings: We have basically had some
initial thoughts provided by some of our own

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.860
staff and other clinicians.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:19.740
I would hope we can include that in the commissioning
work we are doing so that we have a timeline

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:22.890
agreed as part of that work on the longer
term commissioning function.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:27.680
I would see that independent opinion being
part of that.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.720
It would enable people to determine where
they go.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:33.570
However, I should have more detail about that
in the next few weeks.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.430
That will be costed detail, as opposed to
just ideas.

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:36.620
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:37.620
Moira?

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.010
Moira Gibb: Malcolm, thank you.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:45.320
I just wanted to say how good it is to hear
the additional oomph that Jane's leadership

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:46.890
is giving to this.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:50.071
However, we should not begin to underestimate
the scale of change required.

00:09:50.071 --> 00:09:56.630
When we heard about innovative leaders in
our development session yesterday it was about

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:59.580
people on the ground who were very familiar
with it.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:04.410
This is more, kind of, commissioning potentially
feeling like being 'done to'.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:10.240
Therefore, engaging all participants in the
process is really important.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:14.560
However, I do just want to say I was disappointed
that in the whole of the conclusion, those

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.370
three paragraphs, there is no mention of local
government.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:20.410
This has to be a partnership and remain a
partnership.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:24.570
I think the NHS has very significant resources
in it.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:26.070
However, local government also has responsibilities.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:27.070
Jane Cummings: Absolutely.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.820
Moira Gibb: However, I am sure you will do
that.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:32.550
You have mentioned it a number of times; it
is just that they are not there.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:33.890
Jane Cummings: Thank you, Moira.

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.040
I think you are absolutely right.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:39.279
Local government is absolutely critical in
this.

00:10:39.279 --> 00:10:44.710
Certainly, as well as asking for them to be
part of that strategic steering group, we

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:46.260
already work very closely with them.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:49.459
I am already part of a sponsors' group where
LGA are present.

00:10:49.459 --> 00:10:51.730
Therefore, yes, I absolutely agree.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:59.440
Regarding your comment about this not being
'done to' but taking people with you.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.360
Again, you are right.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:07.300
We already have quite a few good examples
of how combined co commissioning, locally,

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:11.600
is having a really positive impact.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:12.660
It is not everywhere.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:14.640
The variation is significant.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:18.580
I suppose one of the ways we are looking at
this is potentially through having some sort

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:19.589
of a national framework.

00:11:19.589 --> 00:11:22.951
However, that would be a framework that could
be locally delivered.

00:11:22.951 --> 00:11:28.080
That would provide people with not only the
skills and experience, but the tools to be

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:29.310
able to do something locally.

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:35.800
It would make sure what they are doing is
really wrapped around every one of those individual

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:36.800
people.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:39.590
The needs will be different.

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:46.290
Therefore, having a national one size fits
all for this group of people is absolutely

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:47.290
wrong.

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:50.680
We would need to make sure it is locally delivered
with local partners.

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:51.680
Malcolm Grant: Good.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:52.680
Victor?

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:57.490
Victor Adebowale: I would like, firstly, to
welcome the approach.

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:02.510
Certainly, the third sector interventions
are attempts to support this.

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:08.910
The papers I have read are very much about
working with, not doing to.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:14.050
To support Moira's point, those papers are
very welcoming and want to engage with local

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:15.050
government.

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:21.930
I do think it is possible to create a national
approach which produces bespoke outcomes.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.720
That is where we need to get to.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:29.100
We need to outcomes to be bespoke to individuals
and their families and communities.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.370
I think it is possible to do that.

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:35.680
I actually welcome the idea of a second opinion.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:41.529
I think one of the barriers has been the sort
of dispersed and sometimes, frankly, confusing

00:12:41.529 --> 00:12:44.650
clinical opinion about safety and whether
people can move.

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:48.600
I think people have a right to seek an independent
view.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:52.610
I am optimistic about accelerating this process.

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:58.971
I am also optimistic about learning from it,
so it becomes embedded in how people with

00:12:58.971 --> 00:13:05.860
learning disabilities receive commissioned
services that they can be involved in.

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:13.339
I think the new era of commissioning is, in
my view, something that needs to move from

00:13:13.339 --> 00:13:17.640
something that is done to people in some mysterious
place to something that they are involved

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:18.640
in.

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:19.640
Otherwise, it is not commissioning.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:23.250
Therefore, I think there is a lot we can learn
from this process, beyond just learning disabilities,

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:24.250
frankly.

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:25.250
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:26.680
Margaret and then Bill.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:28.960
Margaret Casely Hayford: Thank you, Chairman.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:30.240
I will add to that.

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:37.230
I know the collection of data coming out of
the concordat project is going to be transferred

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:38.230
to HSCIC.

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:48.480
However, I think that data, I suppose, appended
to it an ambition so that we are learning

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.450
and we are using the learning.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:59.250
I just think that, as the data is transferred
to HSCIC and we receive reports back, that

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:05.800
aim and ambition should, I suppose, dictate
the way in which we receive the reports and

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:07.450
what we get back from the data.

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:12.760
Jane Cummings: Yes, we can do that.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:21.910
At the moment the data is quarterly and it
contains a huge amount of information.

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:28.360
By the time we get it, it is another two months
post that data.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:31.640
Therefore, as well as looking at transferring
the quarterly through to the HSCIC, we are

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:36.170
also at what we can have that is simpler to
collect but more frequent.

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:41.560
We aim to have some sort of monthly information
to enable us to track.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:45.760
It is absolutely about what we are trying
to deliver and the outcomes we are trying

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:46.760
to achieve.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.170
This is not just about numbers; this is about
people.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:49.170
Margaret Casely Hayford: Yes, exactly.

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:53.220
Jane Cummings: Therefore, it is about how
we can use information to tell us what is

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.240
happening to the people that we are supposed
to be caring for.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:57.140
Margaret Casely Hayford: Thank you.

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:58.290
Malcolm Grant: Noel?

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:04.010
Noel Gordon: I wanted to ask, Jane, whether
you would share the thought that this could

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:08.920
be a very good example of agility and speed
of response.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:17.110
My thought is about the capabilities over
the last year, or two years, in co commissioning

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:20.510
systems and so on.

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:28.760
Is it potentially a good example of how we
could respond quickly to what is clearly a

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:34.350
gap in the system because the capabilities
that have been put in place allow us to do

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:35.350
that?

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:40.550
Examples would be the co commissioning system
and the ecosystem with the third sector.

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:49.180
We have had a report, then having had the
concordat and now establishing a steering

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:52.649
committee to oversee implementation.

00:15:52.649 --> 00:16:02.840
Does that approach respond to the expectations
for speed of response to this situation and

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:03.860
agility?

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:07.660
When do you think we would come up with solutions
and options that could be implemented?

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:13.230
Jane Cummings: To look at your last question
first: are you talking about when we will

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:16.480
come up with solutions around what we are
planning to do next?

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.740
Noel Gordon: From the steering group, I am
thinking, yes.

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:20.250
Long term system options.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:22.079
Jane Cummings: Okay.

00:16:22.079 --> 00:16:26.570
We need to finally agree this.

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:37.019
However, our expectation is that we will have
some initial thoughts and plans set out, certainly,

00:16:37.019 --> 00:16:40.560
during this autumn.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:44.829
As Victor and others have already said, there
is a lot of appetite out there for change.

00:16:44.829 --> 00:16:48.240
There is a lot of expertise within the system.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:54.130
I do not see why we cannot, actually, harness
those expertise, harness that enthusiasm and

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:58.050
actually deliver something reasonably quickly.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:03.519
We certainly want to be in a position where
we can influence the commissioning decisions,

00:17:03.519 --> 00:17:08.290
planning guidance, etc. that we may want to
put in place for next year.

